an  based on the understanding of what  you've given us  we're trying to basically read your mind  at this point in time we're trying to  understand  what you're thinking and how you're  thinking to be able to best design  for you specifically nine times out of  ten it isn't always  perfect straight away because we're only  trying to read your mind we can't  actually read your mind  so what happens here is we come and sit  down with you for the first time  after about a week or so we then discuss  that project and that sketch design with  you  and understand what you like and what  you don't like about it so we can  continue on  with the project so once we've sat down  and discussed that first project  that first attempt from us we'll go back  and begin  developing that sketch design usually  there's either one or two more sketch  designs before we absolutely nail that  brief  and we get exactly what you're looking  for out of that architectural project  once we've completed sketch design we'll  move on to a phase called design  development  design development is where we really  start breaking down the project  and working through it in a more  architectural sense we now start  implementing the council policies the  government policies  and all the local regulations that might  affect this project we take this project  further  until the point where it's ready to  submit to council so  it gets more detailed it gets more  thought out it gets more planned out  and it gets a lot more documentation  behind it towards the end of design  development it's all about understanding  the project a little bit better and  understanding those council policies  so that when we submit to council there  isn't that many  external changes that need to happen  after you get a development approval  this project is usually still very  personal one-on-one with you  we walk you through this process we talk  about it we then generally  discuss and have many many meetings in  between that first sketch design where  it was a stab in the dark  to where we're ready to submit to  council to have a planning approval  so in australia this is considered a  development application where you're  looking to get a development  approval so that way you can actually go  and apply  for a building permit now when we've  submitted to council we can take up to  90 days and just sit there in the  council  yes we can continue to do work on this  project but it's completely up to you  sometimes clients want to push and get  projects done quicker  so we start working on what's called the  working drawings  the working drawing can either be  started once we get development approval  or they can be started simultaneously  once it's at council  it's completely up to you but the  working drawings is where we get down  into the technical details of the house  we start analyzing every connection  point every detail we start selecting  materials  we start selecting plumbing fixtures  taps toilets  showers shower heads and we just go  through the list one by one this is  where you really get involved and you  start to enjoy  and experience the process a lot more  this is where it's all about you it's  all about your selections  or alternatively you can hire an  architect as an interior designer as  well and they can complete that whole  fit out for you the working drawings are  usually a rather large set of documents  there's about 40 or 50 odd pages in  total just of documentation and drawing  there's potentially also another 100 200  300 pages depending on how big the  project is  of actual specifications of everything  that you've selected to go in this house  once this working drawings is completed  we're ready to send it out for tender  now what tender means is we can either  go out and select a number of builders  who would like to tender on this project  and give you their best price  or a number of builders can be invited  there's a whole series of different ways  the general consensus is that we're  going out to builders to be able to get  a quote back  for the project and for it to be very  competitive so you get the best price  possible the tender process is anywhere  from about four to five weeks depending  on how large the project is  it can span longer if it's a busy period  or an extremely large project the  builders will then come in with their  price  usually on the very last minute of the  very last day of the tender process  because they don't want their price to  be leaked to their competitors so  they're very secretive about it even if  they finish three weeks before once all  the tenders have come in we get you back  into the  boardroom usually and we sit down and  discuss  all of the tenders that have come back  in again nine times out of ten the  tenders come in within budget  because we're always budget checking and  budget controlling throughout the whole  project  it's our job to make sure we try and  stick to budget  even though sometimes we go over if that  does happen for example and we do go  over the budget  most of the time it's only five or ten  percent and most clients are okay with  that  if they aren't then we look at ways of  reducing the cost we either  take out some of the specifications pick  some cheaper items or we make the house  smaller  redesign or do whatever we can to bring  that price  and that whole project back into budget  the selection of the builder is then  considered the final point of the tender  process it isn't always  the cheapest builder because when you  review tenders there is  a lot of elements that could be missing  so the cheapest tenderer could be  cheaper  by fifty thousand dollars but they could  have missed some substantial elements in  the build  so the second cheapest builder who has  thoroughly done their research and knows  every bit of this house has potentially  priced it perfectly  whereas now you know that second  cheapest build-up is actually  really the cheapest build-up so the  tender process and  the tender results aren't always fair  and even towards the end  and the bottom line figure isn't always  what it appears to be at the end of the  day it is absolutely the client's choice  so it's your choice  to decide which builder you want to go  for if you want to go for the most  expensive  most premium builder out of all the  tenders who've been in that process  you are absolutely more than welcome to  we only provide recommendations and who  we believe has given the best price and  is the best suited for your project  so at this point in time we reach a fork  in the road it's up to the client to  decide if they want project management  or contract administration from the  architect  or if they wish to part ways usually  most people decide to part ways because  they like to be as heavily involved as  possible with their home  during the construction process now  obviously as an architect i  recommend against this for the simple  fact that you're a lot more emotionally  invested  than any architect who is project  managing your job  so it is our best interest to be able to  produce the best quality at the end of  the day  and make the best possible choices if  you decide to part ways with your  architect  the process has been fairly simple it  has been predominantly around designing  that perfect floor plan  and designing every single element for  you to be able to go out  to a build up with a full set of  documentation that they can build the  house from  if you decide on the other hand to stick  with your architect for the remaining  portion of your contract  well then the architect is very active  and constantly in communication with you  so the architect's role in the project  management or the contract  administration phase  is for them to be able to actually  mediate that process  and to be the middleman so the builder  will always come to the architect and  ask the architect all the questions  first  if the architect for example doesn't  know the question that's when it will be  relayed back to the client  however most of the time all the  questions are simply in the  documentation  that the builder is missed or it's  something that wasn't documented  entirely  for one reason or another and then the  architect can answer that  accordingly the architect usually  manages all the progress claims that  come in for the builder so every time  the builder claims  for a certain value of money the  architect goes out on site  assesses that claim to make sure it's  entirely accurate  and that all the works completed so far  are genuinely up to a high standard and  then they're able  to process that claim if a builder tries  to pull a wool over your eyes and claim  for something that hasn't been completed  well then as the architect as the  mediator you can easily reject that  claim and only pay for a certain portion  of the fees that have been actually  completed this is usually where clients  and builders get into arguments  because clients don't want to pay  something or the builder is trying to  force them to pay something and they  have no leg to stand on  whereas the architect is the mediator  they are the contract administrator  what they say goes if the architect says  no this hasn't been completed therefore  it is not being paid  that's it the client pays for only that  section that has been completed  however this works vice versa as well if  the client is complaining about  something that the builder is wanting to  charge them for  and you go in as the architect and  assess everything and  look at this claim and go well this is a  genuine claim  it needs to be paid for again that is  the final verdict  it's the final thing and the client has  to pay for whatever that architect  has provided that certificate for now  this goes on for the whole construction  process until we get to the very end  where the builder believes they've  reached practical completion practical  completion  is a term in the building industry where  the builder believes they have  reached a stage where the project is  practically complete  so it doesn't necessarily need to be 100  complete but it is  practically there a little scratch here  and there or a little dent that needs to  be fixed up  is definitely not considered as anything  major but no roof couple walls missing  no doors  kitchen not installed things like that  that are pretty major that wouldn't  allow you to move in properly  are not considered practically at this  point in time again we meet with the  builder on site who has your keys ready  to go  as the architect we walk through that  entire project  and look for any defects that the  builder may have missed  may have done incorrectly or anything  that we would consider a defect  defects can be anything from a scratch  on the wall to  actually something completely wrong on  the floor plan once the practical  completion stage has been reached and  this inspection has been completed the  client yourself  takes possession of the site it is your  site now you can  move in you can live in it you can do  whatever you like with that property  now at this point in time it is entirely  your project it is entirely your home  and we step away for approximately 12  months in 12 months time we will come  back  to that site and do a final inspection  it's 12 months generally speaking  because it allows for all four seasons  so if you're off project finish for  example at the end of winter  you'll make sure that you see the next  winter and experience if there's any  leaks  any floods or anything like that that  might occur the final inspection  is there for us again to investigate  that property to understand if anything  has gone wrong if any defects have  occurred  and if all the defects from the previous  inspection have been rectified  after this final inspection that is the  end of the journey with your architect  that is usually where your architect  parts 